; What the symbols of Resolution.eo mean, as a transformation into the deep
; embedding of SMT-LIB that a generated checker's correctness proof is stated
; against.
;
; All three symbols transform pointwise into the SMT-LIB symbol of the same
; name, which is what an arity written on its own says. The SMT-LIB semantics
; this is written against is `smt.eos`, which is the same for every calculus
; over ordinary SMT-LIB and is not this file's to change.
;
; NOT COMPILED HERE. See ../../docs/defining-a-calculus.md, "What was checked
; and what was not": the tutorial's proof tests were run with `ethos`, which
; reads the signature and never this file. This file is written in the form of the
; parent's own `examples/hello/Hello.eos`, with `or` added.

(define-symbol not (x))

(define-symbol and (x y))

(define-symbol or (x y))

; Required whatever the calculus declares: the pipeline asks the semantics which
; datatype names are reserved, even for a calculus with no datatypes, so a
; signature that has none still has to answer. `:keep` is what says the block
; stands regardless of what the input declares. Copied from the parent's
; `examples/hello/Hello.eos`, where the same block appears for the same reason.
(program $eo_to_smt_reserved_datatype_name ((s "String"))
  :keep
  :signature ("String") "Bool"
  (
  (($eo_to_smt_reserved_datatype_name s) ("string_prefix_eq" "str_at_sign" s))
  )
)
